Q: How to create a .snk file from the public key? A: <p>Use the <code>sn -e</code> option. (This extracts it from a dll, which your use of <code>sn -Tp</code> indicates that you have.)</p>
<pre><code>sn -e &quot;file.dll&quot; &quot;PublicKey.snk&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Draw contiguous circles with random radiuses A: <p>To make two circles to touch each other, distance  between centers must be sum of both radiuses. Simple correction (remove <code>if i &gt; 1</code> if shift is needed):</p>

<pre><code>for i:=1 to 10 do begin
  r:=a[i];
  if i &gt; 1 then
      x:=x+r;
  circle(x, y, r);
  x:=x+r;
  end;
end.
</code></pre>

Q: Adding appearance: base-select makes my &lt;select&gt; width vary with the selected &lt;option&gt; A: <p><code>appearance: base-select</code> opts the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> into a new rendering mode (part of the Customizable Select Element proposal, currently Chromium-only). In this mode the element follows standard CSS box model rules, losing the browser's native form-control rendering — including the UA behavior that sized the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> to its widest <code>&lt;option&gt;</code>. The result is a select that resizes with the currently selected option.</p>
<p><code>field-sizing: fixed</code> is designed for <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> — its interaction with <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> under <code>appearance: base-select</code> is not well-specified yet and doesn't reliably restore the old behavior.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Fix 1: CSS grid overlay trick</h2>
<p>Wrap the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> in a grid container. An invisible <code>::after</code> pseudo-element holds the width open to the widest option text, and the select stretches to fill it:</p>
<pre><code>.select-wrap {
  display: inline-grid;
}

.select-wrap::after {
  content: attr(data-widest);
  visibility: hidden;
  height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  white-space: nowrap;
  grid-area: 1 / 1;
  font: inherit;
  padding: inherit;
}

.select-wrap select {
  grid-area: 1 / 1;
  appearance: base-select;
  width: 100%;
}

&lt;div class=&quot;select-wrap&quot; data-widest=&quot;Slow-Roasted Tomato Sauce&quot;&gt;
  &lt;select&gt;
    &lt;option&gt;Braised Short Ribs&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;option&gt;Slow-Roasted Tomato Sauce&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;option&gt;Overnight Sourdough&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;/select&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Since your options are dynamic, compute <code>data-widest</code> in JS after they're built:</p>
<pre><code>function lockSelectWidth(select) {
  const span = document.createElement('span');
  span.style.cssText =
    'position:absolute;visibility:hidden;white-space:nowrap;' +
    'font:' + getComputedStyle(select).font;
  document.body.appendChild(span);

  let max = 0;
  for (const opt of select.options) {
    span.textContent = opt.text;
    max = Math.max(max, span.offsetWidth);
  }

  document.body.removeChild(span);
  select.style.minWidth = max + 'px';
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Fix 2: <code>appearance: none</code> as a simpler fallback</h2>
<p>If you don't need the full base-select feature set yet, <code>appearance: none</code> removes the native chrome without losing the width calculation in most browsers:</p>
<pre><code>select {
  appearance: none;
  min-width: min-content;
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Browser support note</h2>
<p><code>appearance: base-select</code> is still experimental and Chromium-only. Wrap it in <code>@supports</code> so other browsers degrade gracefully:</p>
<pre><code>@supports (appearance: base-select) {
  select,
  ::picker(select) {
    appearance: base-select;
  }
}
</code></pre>
